!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/fixtures/validate-identifier.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/38/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/38/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  The identifier family ([TSON-SCHEMA] §4.2): `identifier_type` composes `text_type`, so an identifier
  takes the text facets, each applied inside [TSON-DATA] §7.7's grammar. Reached here both ways: by
  refining an `identifier` this schema declares -- core declares none, leaving the name free to a schema
  that imports it -- and by constructing the family directly. A naming convention is a
  `pattern` and a closed vocabulary of names is `members`. A value is judged by the grammar first -- a
  token outside it is not an identifier, whatever the facets would say -- and by the facets after.

  `identifier_type` is a UAX #31 profile, and `kebab_name` constructs another one: `-` is medial, so it
  joins two words and never leads, trails or doubles, where the kernel's `identifier` holds `-` as a
  Continue character and admits `content-`.

  A value of an identifier family is a name ([TSON-DATA] §8.2), and the keys of `handlers` are one
  naming scope ([TSON-SCHEMA] §11.4); the keys of `labels` are text, which is data. The elements of
  `roles`, a set of names, are one scope too; those of `path`, an array that admits repetition, are not.
  """
{
  identifier => !identifier_type { continue_add: "-" }
  snake_name => !identifier ^ { pattern: "[a-z][a-z0-9_]*" }
  direction  => !identifier_type { members: [north south] }
  kebab_name => !identifier_type { medial: "-" }

  route => {
    name:    snake_name
    heading: direction
  }

  header => {
    name: kebab_name
  }

  handlers => { identifier => text }
  labels   => { text => text }
  roles    => set<identifier>
  path     => [identifier]
}
